A||||4||Giles||Header question||||1||
Z||000000||Giles||02-21-2000||05:18 AM||||I'm looking at a header for my '84 four cylinder and i came across a header described as a tri-Y header.  What does that mean?  Does it have any significance performance-wise?||||reg||1
Z||000001||1FST2M6||02-21-2000||11:16 AM||||clyinder 1&2 ar connected (1-Y) Cylinder 3&4 are connected (2-Y) and the two ends of 1,2-3,4 are connected making the 3rd Y... then in to the rest of the exhaust system....||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phaeton||02-21-2000||11:19 AM||||A tri-y header matches up cylinders with firing order 180 degree apart and pairs the tubes from them.  Then take those two tube and pair them together.  Leaving three pairs of tube intersections, hence tri-y.  Generally this is done to improve low end torque that headers are famous for taking away.  Header design is highly flammable topic material.<BR>But in general smaller diameter headers with long (30"+) tubes do better at low rpm than large short ones.  Most commercially available headers are one size fits all and will help your engine breathe better than stock. <A HREF="http://www.headersbyed.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.headersbyed.com/</A> <BR>This site sells parts to build your own headers, but lots of information is given on how headers work.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||02-21-2000||01:31 PM||||How do they do tri-Y for the 2.5? Don't #2 and #3 share one big port?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000004||Giles||02-21-2000||08:39 PM||||They do it somehow.  the 2 and 3 ports aren't directly paired.  they do have a divider in between them that includes the head bolt hole.  I think it's possible to do a try-Y on that engine.  It must be, because they're selling it.  I read somewhere on the internet about someone with a tri-Y and started looking for one.  He said that it improved performance considerably. ||||reg||1
